\section{Start here}{

The example below loads synthetic ratings, fits an RSM, saves its summary
and draws a Wright map. Each input row is one rating; \code{person}, \code{facets}
and \code{score} identify columns. The summary's overview tables describe
distributions; \code{as.data.frame(fit)} gives individual estimates.

Before adapting the example, declare the rubric and review category use,
retained rows and assignments with \code{\link[=describe_mfrm_data]{describe_mfrm_data()}}. Unassigned cells
are not zero scores. \code{category_policy = "preserve"} retains the declared
ladder; the default can collapse gaps. Ordinary RSM/PCM MML defaults to a
fixed N(0,1) Person distribution. Estimating its mean/SD requires an explicit
population model. These choices change the model, not just its labels.
See \link{fit_mfrm_technical_details} for model, estimator and constraint choices.
}

\section{From the first summary to diagnostics}{

For supported ordinary RSM/PCM MML/JML fits, continue to
\code{\link[=mfrm_results]{mfrm_results()}} and inspect \code{summary(res)$triage}; \code{\link[=mfrm_report]{mfrm_report()}} and
\code{\link[=export_mfrm_results]{export_mfrm_results()}} use that result bundle. \code{summary(fit)} alone does
not compute diagnostics. Read \code{Why} and \code{NextAction} when inference is
unavailable: it may require a precision review, rather than indicate a
failed point estimate. Keep diagnostics with the fit that produced them.

Other estimators have distinct result classes and downstream support:
\itemize{
\item \strong{CML:} use its structural locations/steps and \code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}}.
Person abilities, population SD and ordinary fit diagnostics are not returned.
Comparison-only fitting reports the requested identifiable contrasts.
\item \strong{GPCM:} consult \code{\link[=gpcm_capability_matrix]{gpcm_capability_matrix()}} before requesting ordinary-model reports.
One-family and two-family routes have different restrictions.
\item \strong{Corrected JML, random-rater and testlet models:} use the methods
documented for their result classes, including \code{\link[=mfrm_response_diagnostics]{mfrm_response_diagnostics()}}
where supported. A shared class name alone is not a capability guarantee.
}
}

\section{Continuing an analysis}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Plan an assessment:} the G-theory tutorial and \code{\link[=mfrm_g_study]{mfrm_g_study()}} /
\code{\link[=mfrm_d_study]{mfrm_d_study()}} specify score components, crossed/nested designs and future
replications. Design identification and interval availability are separate.
\item \strong{Link calibrations or review DFF:} \link{mfrmr_linking_and_dff} distinguishes
joint calibration, fixed anchors, drift and conditional bias screens.
\item \strong{Review missing scores:} \code{\link[=review_mfrm_imputations]{review_mfrm_imputations()}} checks supplied
completions; \code{\link[=fit_mfrm_imputed]{fit_mfrm_imputed()}} retains each fit/failure and
\code{\link[=pool_mfrm_imputed]{pool_mfrm_imputed()}} documents pooling eligibility. Missing scores on
assigned ratings are different from unassigned cells.
\item \strong{Explore external attributes:} \code{\link[=mfrm_features]{mfrm_features()}} reviews the selected
variables; PCA and clustering describe those attributes, not latent abilities.
\item \strong{Report or visualize results:} \link{mfrmr_reports_and_tables} covers saved
output; \link{mfrmr_visual_diagnostics} helps select a figure. The reporting
tutorial provides the complete manuscript workflow.
}

Use \code{\link[=mfrmr_output_guide]{mfrmr_output_guide()}} for specialist routes such as population
prediction, screening evaluation, operational review or model comparison.
Each route's help gives its inputs and limits; running all routes is not a
requirement. \link{mfrmr_compatibility_layer} covers old names and saved analyses.
}

\section{Installed help and online articles}{

Five core tutorials are included with the package. Specialist articles are
online; they cannot all be opened with \code{vignette()}. Match the website's
version to \code{packageVersion("mfrmr")}; installed help describes this version.
}

\examples{
# Load the package
library(mfrmr)

# Load example ratings and look at the first six rows
ratings <- load_mfrmr_data("example_operational")
head(ratings)

# Fit the model
fit <- fit_mfrm(
  data = ratings,
  person = "Person",
  facets = c("Rater", "Criterion"),
  score = "Score",
  rating_min = 1, rating_max = 4, category_policy = "preserve",
  method = "MML",
  model = "RSM",
  population_formula = NULL # Fixed N(0,1) ability distribution
)

# Read the interpretation status before examining or plotting estimates
results <- summary(fit)
results$decision
results$person_overview # One row summarizing person ability estimates
results$facet_overview  # One row per facet: number of levels, mean, SD, range

# Plot the results (Wright map) within the reported interpretation limits
plot(fit)
}
